r this program and our  regulations follow  pretty much to the letter the  requirements that the usda has put in  place and that the farm bill put in  place so we don't have a whole lot of  uh superfluous regulations uh in there  we didn't regulate for fun we regulated  exactly what we needed to do  those regulations are also found on our  website  we're we're really happy to have the  regulations in place because as many of  you know in the past you had to do the  research partner agreement which was  more or less a contract between you and  the state and it outlined what you were  responsible for and what we were  responsible for but there was still some  room for ambiguity in there the rags  really have done away with that  ambiguity so we're pretty happy about  that and it hopefully should add clarity  and certainty for all of our growers  so new for the new  in 2022 under the usda approved program  in addition to submitting the  application to us and the 500  application fee you also have to submit  an fbi background check this is a  federal requirement  the background check can't be any more  than 60 days old at the time when you're  submitting it  and the only thing that really will  disqualify you on that background check  from getting a license is a drug related  felony conviction within the last 10  years  i've seen a lot of rap sheets it's  unbelievable  but as long as you don't have a drug  conviction you're all set  got lots of convictions but  no no felony drug convictions in the  program  also new this year total thc is the  standard that's also a federal  requirement  so we're testing for total thc and the  standard is still 0.3 percent  we will not be the primary sampler or  tester as we have been in the past  so growers that are licensed in the  program now will have to contract with a  certified sampling agent we have a list  of those folks on our website  with chuck schmidt is with me here today  he coordinates how many  is it on here  we have probably  60 i believe uh certified sampling  agents and we have 22 labs identified  they're listed on our website too but  that is a  model that we had to to enter into  because the usda requires now that every  lot of hemp is sampled  and that was just more than than we  could do with the staff that we had so  we had to go to this privatized model  which is what the usda does in states  where they administer the program  you cannot sample your own crop so you  can't be a sampling agent  and a grower  and growers can't  can't sample their friends crops so  nobody who's got a license can be a  grower and no employee  of a licensed grower can be a sampler  also new this year and this is fairly  standard practice with most farm  operations but  a lot of folks that are growing hemp  have not done other farming you need to  register your fields with the fsa the  farm service agency in the county  that you are growing in or the county  close by not every county has an fsa  office anymore but the fields need to be  registered with the fsa and then they  report those locations to the usda who  in turn reports them to the dea the drug  enforcement agency so  this is still  a highly regulated plant and  [Music]  hopefully  that may change in the in the future  so here's some reasons that  an application may be denied if it's in  incorrect or incomplete  the drug-related felony that i mentioned  before  if you don't submit the background check  that's a problem  if you're growing on a field that's  registered to someone else we can't we  can't have that because if  if if that field goes hot and you've got  two people registered there they're  gonna do like this who's responsible for  the hot stuff so  you cannot have co-located  registered field  you cannot grow in an area that's zoned  residential and you cannot grow in a  residence or in an attached garage i  have a  you wouldn't believe how many people  want to grow in their bedrooms it's  very  surprising to me but it does 