le to reduce the  burden of sampling and testing by doing  performance base sampling and that would  exempt some people from sampling if  they're growing varieties that are known  to to be  compliant don't have a tendency to go  hot it's also going to um you know fiber  and grain varieties typically are not  going to  be as likely to go hot as cbd so that  would be a factor that would enter into  it  if you're using certified seed that  would certainly help and the history of  the grower would help us make that  determination as to whether or not you  could be exempted if you've been a  compliant grower you've got all your  paperwork filed that that would  really work in your favor  okay so far this year because the  program expired at the end of last year  uh we at that time we were up to 70 uh  yeah 745 growers that represented about  30 000 acres of registered land but  everyone's license expired so we started  from scratch  we now have 229 licensed growers with a  little over 4 500 acres  outdoors and 2.5 million square feet  indoors registered  and as i mentioned before we have the 60  certified sampling agents and 22  identified labs  so  questions regarding marijuana and cbd  processing or sales  are not  me we do not do that at ag and markets  so that's those should be addressed to  the office of cannabis management and  pat will be following me here shortly so  he can uh  help you out a little bit if you have  questions but there's their information  if you have marijuana related  questions i can leave that up for a  minute  and here's  if you do have questions about hemp  there's our website our industrial hemp  email address and the hemp hotline  so feel free  it's a red phone  no no right exactly  so if you do have questions please you  know feel free to uh  to call anytime and we'll see if we can  help you out  and that's the end of that so i can take  questions now if i don't get to your  question or if you'd rather not ask it  in public i'm going to b